// CallStaticVoidMethod / ...V / ...A (slots 141/142/143, offsets 0x234/0x238/0x23c).
//
// ALL THREE WERE SILENT NO-OPS until v1.0.805. The offsets were mapped to
// JM_CallStaticVoidMethod, but no forwarder was ever registered for it, and
// init_forwarders() fills the whole table with Stub_NotImplemented -- which
// takes four uint32s, ignores them and returns 0. So for an ARM32 guest, EVERY
// void static Java call simply did not happen, with no log line and no error.
//
// That is the worst failure shape we have: a void method is called purely for
// its SIDE EFFECT, so "returned 0" is indistinguishable from success at the
// call site and the damage only shows up much later somewhere else. In HCR
// 1.8.1 the visible consequence was that settings never persisted --
// cocos2d-x's CCUserDefaultAndroid::setBoolForKey / setIntegerForKey / flush
// all funnel into CallStaticVoidMethod on
// org.cocos2dx.lib.Cocos2dxUserDefault, so UserDefault.xml stayed 0 bytes
// forever while every read fell back to its compiled-in default.
//
// All three mirror the Object/Boolean forwarders: parse the cached signature,
// pack the guest arguments into a real jvalue[], call the JVM's A form.
// The three differ only in WHERE the arguments live:
//   non-V (0x234): first variadic arg in r3, the rest on the lifted stack
//                  at g_jni_dispatch_sp+0/4/... -> pack_arm32_inline_args_*
//   V     (0x238): r3 is a POINTER to an AAPCS32 va_list -> pack_arm32_valist_*
//   A     (0x23c): r3 is a guest jvalue[] -> pack_jvalue_array_from_guest
